n common\ncharacteristics such as: Race, ex: ˙m.\u001a' P AK (“yA\nznjy” – “O Negro”); Ethnicity, ex. ÄAm.\u001a' B@ ÄQÆÀ@\n(“Alfrs AlAnjAs” – “Impure Persians”); Group or","source":"2004.02192.pdf","page":3,"score":0.08,"documentId":"03d291f5-64a2-4b85-b166-77c7278070bc","chunkIndex":29},{"text":"gar and hate speech and vulgarity and hate speech\nare not mutually exclusive, a tweet can be just of-\nfensive or offensive and vulgar and/or hate speech.\nThe annotation adhered to the following guidelines:\nOFFENSIVE (OFF): Offensive tweets contain\nexplicit or implicit insults or attacks against other\npeople, or inappropriate language, such as:\nDirect threats or incitement, ex:\n\u0010ÈìPA™÷œ@ \u0010H@Q\u0010Æ” @Ò\u0010ØQk@ (“AHrqwA mqrAt\nAlmEArDp” – “burn opposition headquar-","source":"2004.02192.pdf","page":3,"score":0.07987711,"documentId":"03d291f5-64a2-4b85-b166-77c7278070bc","chunkIndex":27},{"text":"plan to explore other methods for identifying offen-\nsive tweets with greater stylistic diversity.\n3.2 Annotating Tweets\nWe developed annotation guidelines jointly with\nan experienced annotator, who is a native Arabic\nspeaker with good knowledge of various Arabic di-\nalects, in accordance to the OffensEval2019 guide-\nlines. Tweets were given one or more of the fol-\nlowing four labels: offensive, vulgar, hate speech,\nor clean. Since the offensive label covers both vul-","source":"2004.02192.pdf","page":3,"score":0.07549858,"documentId":"03d291f5-64a2-4b85-b166-77c7278070bc","chunkIndex":26},{"text":"Davison, April Kontostathis, and Lynne Edwards.\n2009. Detection of harassment on web 2.0. Pro-\nceedings of the Content Analysis in the WEB, 2:1–7.\nMarcos Zampieri, Shervin Malmasi, Preslav Nakov,\nSara Rosenthal, Noura Farra, and Ritesh Kumar.\n2019. Semeval-2019 task 6: Identifying and cate-\ngorizing offensive language in social media (offen-\nseval). arXiv preprint arXiv:1903.08983.\nMarcos Zampieri, Preslav Nakov, Sara Rosenthal, Pepa\nAtanasova, Georgi